<!doctype html>
{% comment %}
  ============================================================================
  layout/suf.liquid — the layout for rebuilt pages.
  ============================================================================

  Selected per template, not globally. A JSON template opts in with a top-level
  key; a Liquid template with a tag:

      { "layout": "suf", "sections": { ... } }        <- JSON
      {% layout 'suf' %}                              <- Liquid

  templates/password.json already uses the JSON form for layout/password.liquid,
  which is the proof this works on a Vintage theme.

  WHAT THIS LAYOUT LOADS, AND WHY
  -------------------------------
  Net-new:  suf.css, suf.js.
  Legacy:   NONE. As of 2026-08-23 this layout loads no inherited CSS and no
            inherited JavaScript. Everything it serves is suf.css and suf.js.

            The last to go were dt-framework.css and dt-custom.css. By then
            they were supplying a reset rather than component styles, so
            frontend/styles/components/_suf-base.scss carries a small
            compatibility block standing in for the parts the forked header's
            markup depends on. That block is deleted with the header.

  NOT loaded, deliberately: jQuery, dt-framework.css, dt-custom.css,
  dt-plugins.js (Swiper), dt-theme.js, handlebars, select2 and its stylesheet,
  ajax-cart, lazysizes, swiper-bundle.min.css, dT_main.js and dT_main_app.js.
  That is roughly 850 KB that theme.liquid loads on every page and this layout
  does not.

  Consequences, all intended:

  * NO CART DRAWER. The header cart icon falls back to its own href and goes to
    the cart page. New pages link straight to checkout, so the drawer is not
    wanted. The mobile NAV drawer still works, because its code is inline in
    the header section rather than in any file dropped above.
  * NO LAZYSIZES. Use native loading="lazy", not class="lazyload".
  * NO COOKIE BANNER yet. See docs/migration.md — resolve before any suf page
    is assigned to a live page handle.
  * Legacy sections other than the forked header render only as well as
    dt-framework.css happens to style them. Prefer suf-* sections here.

  The legacy block below is temporary and shrinks as sections/suf-header.liquid
  is reduced. Do not add to it — port the section instead. The reduction
  checklist is in docs/migration.md.
{% endcomment %}
<html lang="{{ request.locale.iso_code }}">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <meta name="theme-color" content="{{ settings.primary_color }}">

    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://cdn.shopify.com" crossorigin>

    {%- if canonical_url != blank -%}
      <link rel="canonical" href="{{ canonical_url }}">
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if settings.favicon != blank -%}
      <link rel="shortcut icon" href="{{ settings.favicon | image_url: width: 32 }}" type="image/png">
    {%- endif -%}

    {% render 'SEO-tags' %}
    {% render 'social-meta-tags' %}

    {% comment %}
      TWO of the five faces, not all five. snippets/suf-fonts.liquid declares
      400, 600, 700, 800 and 900; @font-face is lazy, so the other three cost
      nothing until a page actually renders something at that weight.

      Preloading is not lazy -- it fetches at high priority before the browser
      knows whether anything needs the file -- so only what paints on first
      load belongs here:

        400  body copy.
        800  every heading, and 51 of the 67 weight declarations in suf.css.

      Deliberately NOT preloaded, and each verified rather than assumed:

        600  mega-menu column headings and the mobile drawer's nav links.
             Both render only after a menu is opened.
        700  8 uses, all below the fold -- the result chip, a dark-band <em>,
             table cells, contact, seminars.
        900  2 uses, on two pages.

      Preloading those three cost ~41 KB of contended bandwidth on every page
      and earned Chrome's "preloaded but not used within a few seconds"
      warning on most of them. 27 KB for the pair that is genuinely needed.
    {% endcomment %}
    {{ 'hanken-grotesk-400.woff2' | asset_url | preload_tag: as: 'font', type: 'font/woff2', crossorigin: 'anonymous' }}
    {{ 'hanken-grotesk-800.woff2' | asset_url | preload_tag: as: 'font', type: 'font/woff2', crossorigin: 'anonymous' }}


    {{ 'suf.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

    {% comment %}
      Theme-editor values -> custom properties, after suf.css so they override
      the fallbacks compiled into it. Two separate sets on purpose: the brand
      palette for new components, and the inherited palette the forked header
      still needs. See each snippet for why they cannot be one.
    {% endcomment %}
    {% render 'suf-tokens' %}
    {% render 'suf-legacy-tokens' %}

    {% comment %}
      Hanken Grotesk is applied on this layout only. theme.liquid stays on
      Arial permanently -- that is a settled decision, not a pending task, and
      the reasoning is in snippets/suf-fonts.liquid. The legacy templates have
      rendered in Arial for years and are being replaced rather than restyled;
      the header opts back in for itself because it is shared.

      set_weights: false because this layout supplies its own weights from
      suf.css. The snippet's `h1, h2, ...` rule is (0,0,1); the suf base layer
      is `:where(.suf-body) h1`, which is ALSO (0,0,1) since :where() adds
      nothing -- and this renders after suf.css, so the tie went to the
      snippet and held every heading at 700 while the stylesheet asked for
      800. The family rules keep their !important; those still do real work.
    {% endcomment %}
    {% render 'suf-fonts', apply: true, set_weights: false, family: "'Hanken Grotesk', Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" %}

    {% # theme-check-enable ParserBlockingScript %}


    {% comment %}
      Required. Shopify injects analytics, app embeds and the theme editor
      bridge here. Removing it breaks the editor and app blocks.
    {% endcomment %}
    {{ content_for_header }}
  </head>

  <body class="suf-body template-{{ template | replace: '.', ' ' | truncatewords: 1, '' | handle }}">
    <a class="suf-skip-link" href="#suf-main">Skip to content</a>

    {% comment %}
      The back-to-top link's target. A real element rather than href="#",
      which scrolls the viewport but leaves keyboard focus at the bottom of
      the page -- the next Tab then resumes in the footer, which is the
      opposite of what the control promises. tabindex="-1" makes it focusable
      by the fragment jump without putting it in the tab order.
    {% endcomment %}
    <span id="suf-top" tabindex="-1"></span>

    {% comment %}
      The reveal threshold, as an element. assets/suf-totop.js watches it with
      an IntersectionObserver: while it is in view the page is inside the first
      800px and the button stays hidden. Its height is the threshold, so the
      distance is set once in CSS rather than duplicated in JS.
    {% endcomment %}
    <span class="suf-totop__sentinel" aria-hidden="true"></span>

    {% comment %}
      The rebuilt header. See docs/header-rebuild.md -- this is step 3, and
      reverting it means restoring the four lines below and nothing else:

        <div class="mobile-menu" data-menu="dt-main-menu" id="dt-main-menu-panel"></div>
        <div class="mobile-menu-overlay"></div>
        {% raw %}{% section 'suf-header' %}{% endraw %}

      Those two empty divs were scaffolding the forked header's inline JS
      filled with the mobile menu; suf-nav carries its own panel, so they went
      with it. sections/suf-header.liquid is untouched on disk.

      layout/theme.liquid still renders sections/header.liquid. Pointing it
      here is step 5, after this one has been lived with.
    {% endcomment %}
    {% section 'suf-nav' %}

    <main id="suf-main" class="suf-main">
      {{ content_for_layout }}
    </main>

    {% section 'suf-footer' %}

    {% comment %}
      Back to top. Always visible, as in the mockup -- the legacy snippet
      revealed it on scroll with jQuery, which this layout does not load. If it
      should be revealed rather than persistent, that is a few lines in suf.js,
      not a reason to keep the old snippet.

      Styled provisionally: it takes the CTA colour so it moves with the rest
      of the chrome when the header and footer are rebuilt.
    {% endcomment %}
    <a class="suf-totop" href="#suf-top">
      <span class="suf-totop__arrow" aria-hidden="true">&uarr;</span>
      <span class="suf-visually-hidden">Back to top</span>
    </a>


    {% comment %} Net-new SUF scripts. Native ES module; type=module implies defer. {% endcomment %}
    <script src="{{ 'suf.js' | asset_url }}" type="module"></script>
  </body>
</html>
